(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacodynamics-exam-2-pharmacodynamics-master-exam-version-2"]={"title":"Pharmacodynamics Comprehensive Quiz — Set 2","category":"Pharmacodynamics","questions":[{"q":"Pharmacokinetics is best defined as:","choices":["The effect of the drug on the body","The effect of the body on the drug (ADME)","The study of drug-receptor binding affinity","The measurement of therapeutic index"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pharmacokinetics is what the body does to the drug — absorption, distribution, metabolism, elimination (ADME). — PK I & II, Slide 4"},{"q":"Carrier-mediated (special carrier) transport differs from passive diffusion in that it:","choices":["Always follows the concentration gradient downhill","Is governed by Fick's law","Can be saturated and may move drug against a gradient","Requires no cellular energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Special carriers are capacity-limited (saturable), can move against a gradient, and require energy. — PK I & II, Slide 8, 20"},{"q":"When the pH of a solution equals a drug's pKa, the drug is:","choices":["50% protonated and 50% unprotonated","Fully un-ionized","Fully ionized","Precipitated out of solution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At pH = pKa, the molecule is 50% protonated and 50% unprotonated by definition. — PK I & II, Slide 14"},{"q":"A weak base in an acidic environment will predominantly be:","choices":["Protonated, charged, and lipid-insoluble","Unprotonated, uncharged, and lipid-soluble","Neutral and freely diffusible","Converted to a weak acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A weak base in acid gains a proton to become BH+ — charged and lipid-insoluble (e.g., protonated morphine is less lipid-soluble). — PK I & II, Slide 18"},{"q":"Endocytosis is the appropriate absorption mechanism for a substance that is:","choices":["Small and lipophilic","Too large, lipid-insoluble, or impermeant","A weak acid at low pH","A gas that is exchanged in the lung"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endocytosis engulfs substances too large, lipid-insoluble, or impermeant (e.g., B12 with intrinsic factor, iron with transferrin). — PK I & II, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 70 kg man is given 100 mg of a drug with Vd = 0.6 L/kg IV. What is his estimated plasma concentration?","choices":["Approximately 2.4 mg/L","Approximately 1.4 mg/L","Approximately 0.6 mg/L","Approximately 6.0 mg/L"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vd = 70 × 0.6 = 42 L; C0 = 100 mg ÷ 42 L ≈ 2.38 mg/L. — PK I & II, Slide 31"},{"q":"A drug with a very HIGH volume of distribution is most likely:","choices":["Hydrophilic and confined to plasma","Lipophilic and distributed widely into tissue","Highly protein-bound in the vasculature","Restricted by dehydration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A high Vd usually indicates a lipophilic drug distributed extensively into tissues. — PK I & II, Slide 28-29"},{"q":"Which enzyme is a principal PHASE I metabolizing enzyme?","choices":["UDP-glucuronosyltransferase","Sulfotransferase","Glutathione-S-transferase","Cytochrome P450"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cytochrome P450 is a principal phase I enzyme (along with monoamine oxidase, esterases, etc.). — PK I & II, Slide 38"},{"q":"Phase II metabolic reactions primarily:","choices":["Uncover polar functional groups by oxidation or reduction","Reduce nitro groups","Hydrolyze ester bonds","Add or alter a molecule to increase water solubility via conjugation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Phase II adds/alters a moiety (glucuronidation, sulfation) to increase water solubility for excretion. — PK I & II, Slide 39"},{"q":"Grapefruit juice is described in the lecture as an agent that:","choices":["Inhibits drug metabolism","Induces hepatic metabolism","Increases renal filtration","Enhances first-pass clearance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Grapefruit juice is listed as an inhibitor of drug metabolism (contrast with charcoal-broiled foods, which induce). — PK I & II, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which condition would MOST impair hepatic drug-metabolizing capacity?","choices":["Diabetic nephropathy","Acute renal failure","Renal artery stenosis","Alcoholic cirrhosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alcoholic cirrhosis (a liver disease) impairs hepatic drug-metabolizing enzymes. — PK I & II, Slide 50-51"},{"q":"In aspirin (a weak acid) overdose, alkalinizing the urine with sodium bicarbonate enhances elimination by:","choices":["Increasing the tubular reabsorption of aspirin back into blood","Ion trapping — keeping aspirin charged so it is not reabsorbed","Inhibiting hepatic metabolism of aspirin","Reducing glomerular filtration of the drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Raising urine pH increases the charged fraction of the weak acid, trapping it in the tubule (ion trapping) and preventing reabsorption. — PK I & II, Slide 57"},{"q":"For a first-order drug, the fraction eliminated per unit time is:","choices":["A constant amount regardless of concentration","A constant percentage, proportional to concentration","Fixed in absolute amount per hour","Zero until a threshold is reached"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-order elimination removes a constant percentage (e.g., 15%/hr), so the absolute amount tracks concentration. — PK I & II, Slide 63, 66"},{"q":"The half-life equation t½ = (0.693 × Vd)/Cl predicts that decreasing clearance will:","choices":["Prolong the half-life","Shorten the half-life","Not change the half-life","Reduce the volume of distribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A smaller Cl increases half-life because clearance is in the denominator. — PK I & II, Slide 72-73"},{"q":"The maintenance dose can be calculated by rearranging Css = Dose/(Cl × interval) to:","choices":["Dose = Cl / (Css × interval)","Dose = Css / (Cl × interval)","Dose = Css × Cl × dosing interval","Dose = Css × Vd × Ke"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rearranging gives Dose = Css × Cl × dosing interval. — PK I & II, Slide 81"},{"q":"Highly lipid-soluble drugs filtered at the glomerulus tend to be:","choices":["Rapidly reabsorbed back into the circulation","Rapidly and completely excreted","Trapped as charged species in the tubule","Actively secreted without reabsorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lipid-soluble drugs are freely filtered but rapidly reabsorbed; charged molecules are not readily reabsorbed. — PK I & II, Slide 55"},{"q":"Compared with a bactericidal agent, a bacteriostatic antibiotic:","choices":["Directly causes bacterial cell death","Has no measurable post-antibiotic effect by definition","Always requires lower doses","Inhibits a vital growth pathway without directly killing the organism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bacteriostatic agents (e.g., tetracyclines, sulfonamides) inhibit a vital growth pathway but do not directly kill. — Antimicrobials, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is BACTERICIDAL?","choices":["Erythromycin","Vancomycin","Tetracycline","Trimethoprim"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is bactericidal (along with penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, metronidazole). — Antimicrobials, Slide 23"},{"q":"For a time-dependent antibiotic, the pharmacodynamic parameter that best predicts efficacy is:","choices":["Peak concentration well above the MIC","The post-antibiotic effect duration","A single high concentration-dependent peak spike above the MIC","The time that drug concentration remains above the MIC (40-70% of the interval)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Time-dependent killing is optimized when concentration stays above MIC for 40-70% of the dosing interval; continuous/frequent infusions are best. — Antimicrobials, Slide 24"},{"q":"As one advances from 1st to higher cephalosporin generations, the general trend is:","choices":["Increasing gram-negative activity with decreasing gram-positive activity","Increasing gram-positive and decreasing gram-negative activity","Loss of all bactericidal activity","Conversion to bacteriostatic mechanism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Advancing generations gain gram-negative coverage while losing some gram-positive activity (4th gen recovers gram-positive without sacrificing gram-negative). — Antimicrobials, Slide 39-40"},{"q":"Vancomycin's mechanism of action is best described as:","choices":["A glycopeptide that blocks cell wall cross-linking","A beta-lactam that inactivates the PBPs directly","A 30S ribosomal inhibitor","A DNA gyrase inhibitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is a glycopeptide that targets the cell wall by blocking cross-linking, making resistance more difficult to develop. — Antimicrobials, Slide 43"},{"q":"Oral vancomycin is specifically indicated for:","choices":["MRSA bacteremia","Gram-negative UTIs","Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia","Severe C. difficile colitis (not systemically absorbed)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral vancomycin is limited to severe antibiotic-associated C. difficile colitis because it is not orally absorbed and acts locally in the gut. — Antimicrobials, Slide 44"},{"q":"Tetracyclines inhibit bacterial protein synthesis by:","choices":["Binding the 50S subunit to block translocation","Inactivating penicillin-binding proteins","Binding the 30S subunit and preventing tRNA attachment","Inhibiting DNA-dependent RNA polymerase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tetracyclines bind the 30S subunit and prevent tRNA from binding the mRNA-ribosome complex. — Antimicrobials, Slide 48-49"},{"q":"The most common mechanism of tetracycline resistance is:","choices":["Beta-lactamase production","An efflux pump that expels the drug","Altered penicillin-binding proteins","Ribosomal methylation of the 50S subunit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tetracycline resistance occurs primarily via an efflux pump that expels drug from the cell. — Antimicrobials, Slide 49"},{"q":"Macrolides and clindamycin share which target?","choices":["The 30S ribosomal subunit directly","The bacterial cell wall","Bacterial DNA gyrase","The 50S ribosomal subunit (blocking translocation)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrolides and clindamycin inhibit translocation by binding the 50S subunit. — Antimicrobials, Slide 52"},{"q":"Fluoroquinolones such as levofloxacin exert bactericidal activity by:","choices":["Blocking cell wall cross-linking","Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit","Inhibiting DNA gyrase and topoisomerase","Disrupting folate synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase, halting DNA replication and transcription. — Antimicrobials, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone is preferred for community-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Levofloxacin","Nalidixic acid","Moxifloxacin for UTI"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Levofloxacin (with improved gram-positive and Pseudomonas coverage) is used for CAP; ciprofloxacin is not. — Antimicrobials, Slide 60-61"},{"q":"Metronidazole's spectrum centers on:","choices":["Gram-positive aerobes only","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Anaerobes (gram-positive and gram-negative) and parasites","Atypical respiratory tract pathogens"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole covers gram-positive and gram-negative anaerobes plus parasites, with indications like C. difficile and BV/trichomoniasis. — Antimicrobials, Slide 63-64"},{"q":"A disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol is a characteristic adverse effect of:","choices":["Cephalexin","Doxycycline","Metronidazole","Azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole causes a disulfiram-like reaction (flushing, nausea, vomiting, diaphoresis) with ethanol. — Antimicrobials, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which is a recognized general adverse effect of antibiotic use?","choices":["Universal QT interval prolongation across all of the classes","Permanent loss of renal filtration","Disruption of normal flora with C. difficile overgrowth","Irreversible ribosomal damage in humans"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Disruption of normal flora leading to C. diff overgrowth, GI disturbance, and yeast infections is a general adverse effect. — Antimicrobials, Slide 66"}]};
